#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100668 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac31493b-30d0-5ed1-8422-f1f475e3c576" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points that Must Lie on a Translated Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "8c6c748a-f067-527d-91aa-3ead12bb569d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4106" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on $y=f(x)+3$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x)+3\n$$\n\nis obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units *up*.\n\nHence, if the point $(3,4)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, then the point\n\n$$\n(3,4+3) = (3,7)\n$$\n\nlies on\n\n$$\ny=f(x) + 3\n$$\n\n![](images/57/577911187229d6a56a8885e4cea2e25752615ed7bb807768abf4b54c5073d177.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(3,4)$ lies on $y = f(x)$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3) = 4\n$$\n\nNow, if $x = 3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= f(3) + 3 \\\\\n&= 4 + 3 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(3,7)$ must lie on\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100669 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8224846-04e6-531d-9415-9a18d2afddb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Translated Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7b856c88-45bf-54a5-80c4-134a7544742c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4107" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below and the point $(0,9)$ lies on the curve. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)-2$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0c3acc57c74ced95e7387e285d9de1c629e7724fe9e111b13df209198069e8d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y = f(x)$ passes through the point $(0, 9)$ as shown in the diagram.\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,7)$.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27de6227dee35cb8a7eaf2873409dfd6ad8c3c5f8d0cbd0de9ac223925b3fd76.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100670 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10557b22-4b4b-5860-abcb-0dcd96a5743d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertical Shift of a Translated Function"}] :question/id #uuid "85d2f012-deb3-55dc-8e38-51bfe62eab20" :question/math-academy-id "e-4108" :question/problem "The point $A$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$ and the point $B$ with coordinates $(2,-4)$ lies on the curve $y = g(x)$. If the curve $y = g(x)$ is related to $y = f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x) = f(x) + k$, then what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the point $B(2, -4)$ lies $3$ units *below* the point $A(2, -1)$. So, the graph of $y = g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/46/4656ae5ff75132fc9b760b6a8f73b0b434a8f66ecd621fa974a265df1d0d8a2c.png)\n\nAs a result, $k = -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d982b565-2c41-5687-8d00-86b971bfb18b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of an Original Function Given a Translated Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1c57d35a-24ad-51ae-b00d-0f55396f045d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5007" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x) +2$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$?\n\n![](images/f5/f5404d5fbf52098eaa7161f0219e4df98a3ae9f5b6b7b6b0220089d62abff82d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 2\n$$\n\ncan be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *up*.\n\nSo, the graph $y = f(x)$ would be $2$ units *below* the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 2\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0ce67d91fd6c15d97e882c785392f001a4f38ed9bedd50d8631b34e4d5543adc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8224846-04e6-531d-9415-9a18d2afddb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Translated Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99857060-4593-5df7-bb86-6d13bb41052f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5decf42f3a6059de5d197dbab0c82fe37a67f984bd8b2218fd2480d7bab38dcd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098572} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fe9c0b-e773-51cf-8f13-6e347d09961e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94c0b61fb4cc7520c040429baa4b7377ce2705869346d6c2213155ea9557b100.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098573} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bdadae-1e47-5327-b074-0eb473615370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f2/f2ccec17cbdd53865b5915156cced1e5533d18fc0cccda566d942f521118216c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098574} {:answer/id #uuid "ee17b9c8-c085-5838-a10c-520080108563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ffbec42cd49ed7bb3c30b41e3a91b9c3aa592b0017813ea3debfc3e613b61b27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098575} {:answer/id #uuid "fab4872e-1342-5416-9585-f66b6910a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f1a2f1b25cc02927d65d55de971b6579377629b570e08dad80d543a36e405cf9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098576}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3626a35f-e2e4-5cf7-9463-6bab151822cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098571}] :question/id #uuid "c24cb6a5-e9b4-5b2a-b705-fcd1200bf566" :question/math-academy-id "q-15562" :question/problem "![](images/82/82012efd1fbf78238ce1b6ecc49819283976db8f39ac5f57ec232a5eda8029dc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0, 1)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f(x) + 2$?"}]}
